\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

% - FB's manipulation failures are associated with systematic errors in predicted future-state occupancy that obscure the relative value of actions needed for task success.
% - Control bottlenecks amplify FB's successor-estimation errors: these errors are larger around interactions where a restricted set of actions determines subsequent task progress.
% - The normalized parameterization and categorical Bellman objective used by \name{} reduce task-relevant successor-estimation errors and improve action rankings, particularly around the interactions identified in Section~\ref{sec:exp-bottlenecks}.
% - The improvements in successor estimation translate into higher task performance, with larger gains on tasks requiring the interactions for which prediction accuracy improves.
% - The learned successor representation supports useful policy selection for reward functions withheld during pretraining, including objectives that require different preferences over future states.

Our experiments test the argument above in four steps. %%AZ.9.12: be more specific, what argument are you testing? Do you just mean the 3 components you modify? just say that.
%%AZ.9.12: looks like we're actually missing this ablation in the experiments. I think it's important to have something that tests each of the 3 modifications to FB (I guess you might only be able to show this as two modifications, since the 3rd is meant to address the first)
First, we isolate the proposed failure in the controlled bottleneck MDP, where the exact successor measure and action gap are known (\cref{sec:exp-didactic}). %%AZ.9.12: what proposed failure
We then evaluate \name{} on multiple manipulation tasks across benchmarks on OGBench, MetaWorld, and ManiSkill in \cref{sec:exp-manipulation}.
\Cref{sec:reward-transfer} tests whether the same frozen representation remains useful when the downstream reward is specified only after pretraining.
\Cref{sec:exp-failure} examines the OGBench policies more closely to determine where FB fails and whether its successor predictions and action rankings explain those failures.

\paragraph{Experimental protocol.}
All methods use states, are trained for $10^6$ gradient steps on the same fixed offline dataset.
We report results over $5$ independent training seeds.
For offline policy learning, we use the actor from \citet{bagatella-iclr26a}, based on Flow Q-Learning~\citep{park-icml25a}.
Success rates are averaged over the final three evaluations, with $10$ episodes per task.
Full implementation details are given in \cref{app:implementation}.

\paragraph{Baselines.}
\textbf{FB}~\citep{touati-neurips21a,touati-iclr23a} is our primary baseline and directly tests whether normalized SM approximation addresses the failure identified in \cref{sec:failure}.
We compare against \textbf{HILP}~\citep{park-iclr24a} and \textbf{RLDP}~\citep{jajoo-iclr26a} to test whether alternative geometric or predictive representations can avoid the same limitation without explicitly modeling a normalized successor distribution.
% We additionally consider \textbf{TD-JEPA} as an alternative that predicts low-dimensional future features rather than reconstructing future-state occupancy.
Finally, in the bottleneck MDP, we ablate the normalized parameterization and categorical objective separately, and compare against unit-normalized FB, to identify which component of \name{} preserves the control-critical action difference.

\subsection{Preserving the bottleneck decision}
\label{sec:exp-didactic}
%%AZ.9.12: this is the env where you should also conduct the ablations on Density-FB.
At a control bottleneck, it is important for the critic to preserve the value difference between the competing actions.
We test two predictions on the process from \cref{sec:failure} and on five randomly generated bottleneck processes, shown in \cref{app:bottleneck-family}.
First, at a fixed rank, FB should lose the action gap as the bottleneck becomes rarer in the training objective.
Second, increasing the representation rank should eventually recover the missing distinction, and \name{} should require less rank to do so.

\begin{figure*}[t]
    \begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ICLR/figures/rare_bottleneck/fig_rare_bottleneck_lemma.pdf}
        \caption{}
        \label{fig:rare-lemma}
    \end{subfigure}
    \hfill
    \begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ICLR/figures/bottleneck_family/fig_family_gap_two_arm.pdf}
        \caption{}
        \label{fig:family-rank}
    \end{subfigure}
    \captionsetup{skip=2pt}
    \caption{
    \textbf{Control bottlenecks are lost when they are rare and capacity is limited.}
    (Left) At fixed rank, reducing the bottleneck's weight causes FB to lose the decision even though its true action gap is unchanged.
    (Right) Increasing rank eventually lets FB recover the decision, while \name{} requires substantially less capacity.
    }
    \label{fig:bottleneck-main}
\end{figure*}

\paragraph{Does FB lose a decision when the bottleneck becomes rare?}
\Cref{lem:rare-bottleneck} predicts that the consequence of choosing the wrong action at the bottleneck can stay exactly the same even as that decision contributes less and less to FB's global squared objective.
We test this by directly reducing the weight of the bottleneck in the objective (\cref{fig:rare-lemma}).
FB preserves almost the full gap when the bottleneck has enough weight, but it quickly disappears once the bottleneck becomes rare.
At the same rank, the normalized model preserves essentially the full gap at the population optimum throughout these experiments.
Therefore linear squared approximation chooses not to spend its limited capacity on bottlenecks.

\paragraph{How much capacity is needed to keep the decision?}
We therefore vary the representation rank and repeat the experiment across the five generated bottleneck processes (\cref{fig:family-rank}).
Giving FB more rank eventually fixes the problem: it preserves $5\%$, $12\%$ and $38\%$ of the exact action gap at ranks $2$, $3$ and $4$, and needs a median rank of $7$ out of nine states to recover $90\%$ of it.
\name{} preserves $59\%$ at rank $2$ and $91\%$ at rank $3$, at a median rank of $3$.
Therefore, the exact SM contains the information needed to choose the right action.
The failure comes from which parts of that SM are kept when capacity is limited.

\paragraph{Which change in \name{} preserves the decision?}
We next ablate the three design decisions of \name{} on the bottleneck processes (\Cref{fig:ablation}).
Merely normalizing FB's target only rescales the same squared regression problem.
Only once the bilinear score is placed inside a softmax, the gap starts being preserved for both squared loss and cross-entropy.
Thus, the main gain comes from representing the SM as a normalized distribution.
We use the categorical objective because it gives the Bellman update derived in \cref{sec:method}, and because it trains better on the benchmarks of \cref{sec:exp-manipulation}.
The remaining two curves are the controls for the other two changes: reading the softmax model through FB's linear map loses the gap at every rank, so the integration readout is required by the parameterization rather than a separate choice, and smoothing the immediate target caps the preserved gap near $0.71$, which is why the one-hot term is kept.

\begin{figure}[htb]
    \centering
    \begin{minipage}[c]{0.62\textwidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ICLR/figures/bottleneck_family/fig_family_ablation_trained.pdf}
    \end{minipage}\hfill
    \begin{minipage}[c]{0.34\textwidth}
        \captionsetup{justification=raggedright,singlelinecheck=false}
        \caption{\textbf{The normalized parameterization preserves the bottleneck decision.} Fraction of the exact action gap preserved across representation ranks, TD-trained and averaged over the five generated processes; shading is the $95\%$ interval over processes and training runs. Simply rescaling FB's target does not fix the failure. Placing the bilinear score inside a softmax preserves the gap under both squared error and the categorical objective.}
        \label{fig:ablation}
    \end{minipage}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The same structure in manipulation}
\label{sec:exp-failure}

% The improvements in successor estimation translate into higher task performance, with larger gains on tasks requiring the interactions for which prediction accuracy improves.
The bottleneck process is an idealization; we now ask whether the same structure is present in manipulation, before measuring performance.
In cube manipulation the states where the object is under control play the role of the bottleneck: with the cube held, securing and releasing lead to entirely different futures for it, while during free-space motion the cube's future is nearly the same whatever the arm does.
We examine the frozen \texttt{cube-single} and \texttt{cube-double} checkpoints of the runs evaluated in \cref{sec:exp-manipulation} and ask two questions: 
\begin{inparaenum}
    \item where in the interaction does the policy fail? and
    \item does the critic correctly predict the consequences of its actions at those states?
\end{inparaenum}

We first divide each evaluation episode by the first stage at which it fails, following the interaction phases of \citet{mohan-rlbrew26a}: approach, acquisition, maintain, transport, or success.
The left panel of \Cref{fig:failure} shows that FB's failures on \texttt{cube-single} occur predominantly after the cube has been acquired, during maintain and transport.
\name{} fails somewhat more often during acquisition, but rarely fails once the cube is securely held.
On \texttt{cube-double}, both methods fail more often overall, but FB's failures are again concentrated after acquisition.
This places the main performance gap at the interactions highlighted by the bottleneck argument in Section~\ref{sec:failure}.

We next test whether the critic is inaccurate at these states.
From the same rollouts, we restore recorded simulator states from three stages of the interaction, approach, in-hand, and carrying, and compare each critic's predicted distribution over future cube positions with rollouts of its own policy.
We also evaluate a common set of candidate actions at each state and compare the critic's action ranking with their Monte-Carlo values.
Full details of the restored-state protocol and candidate actions are given in \aditya{Add Appendix}

The right panels of \Cref{fig:failure} show that FB's predicted cube distribution remains close to the dataset distribution across all three state types and differs substantially from the futures realized by its current policy.
Its ranking of candidate actions is also close to chance, including at carrying states where the choice of action determines whether the object remains secured.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \begin{subfigure}[b]{0.46\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ICLR/figures/failure/fig_failure_where.pdf}
        \caption{Where the failure occurs.}
        \label{fig:failure-where}
    \end{subfigure}\hfill
    \begin{subfigure}[b]{0.53\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{ICLR/figures/failure/fig_failure_why.pdf}
        \caption{Whether the failure is in the prediction.}
        \label{fig:failure-why}
    \end{subfigure}
    \caption{
    \textbf{Diagnosing FB's manipulation failure.}
    (a) The first failed interaction phase for evaluation episodes from the $10^6$-step checkpoints evaluated in \cref{sec:exp-manipulation}.
    (b) Total-variation distance between predicted and realized future cube-position distributions (top) and rank correlation between predicted and Monte-Carlo action values (bottom), evaluated at restored simulator states from different interaction stages.
    Error bars show standard errors over states.
    }
    \label{fig:failure}
\end{figure}

