If the bottleneck failure identified above contributes to manipulation performance, then improving the successor estimate should improve control on tasks that require precise interactions with objects.
We test this first on OGBench, where FB, \name{}, HILP~\citep{park-iclr24a}, and RLDP~\citep{jajoo-iclr26a} use the same offline data and evaluation protocol, against the goal-conditioned reference implementations of \citet{park2024ogbench}. %%AZ.9.12: please include a baselines section that explains these baselines and why they were chosen.

\Cref{tab:manipulation} gives every method the task as a goal observation.
\name{} improves over FB from $17.3$ to $84.3$ on \texttt{cube-single}, from $3.3$ to $21.0$ on \texttt{cube-double} and from $24.7$ to $63.7$ on \texttt{scene}, and it is the strongest method on \texttt{scene} overall.
HILP is the stronger successor representation on \texttt{cube-single} and \texttt{cube-double} under this interface, and GCIQL remains ahead on \texttt{cube-double} and \texttt{puzzle-3x3}.
All successor-based methods remain near floor on \texttt{puzzle-3x3}, indicating that changing the successor objective alone does not solve every source of difficulty in manipulation.
\Cref{app:reward-extraction} repeats the comparison with the task given as a reward, which is the interface \cref{sec:method} is built for and the one on which the four successor methods separate most clearly.

